BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Branch Art Exhibit: Works by Natalia Ionescu
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260702T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260730T235959
UID:6a35437ff56bd86e00b7bfdc@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Throughout the month of July\, works by artist Natalia Ionescu
  are on display at Agincourt Branch.\nNatalia is passionate about finding
  beauty and inspiration in all the people\, places and situations that she
  encounters in her everyday life. She loves sharing with others what th
 e world looks like through her lens in a way that might brighten up their 
 day\, and contributing to our community in a meaningful way.\nNatalia is a
 n immigrant artist from Eastern Europe who studied design there. She arriv
 ed to Canada as an adult and built a new life here together with her fami
 ly. She spent a few years in the social work field\, and is currently wor
 king in the corporate world.\nThis exhibit is part of TPL's\nArt Exhibits 
 Space\nprogram. Toronto Public Library provides space for local artists to
  exhibit their work at locations across the city. These exhibits aim to re
 flect the diverse cultural interests of the city and its neighbourhoods.\n
 The exhibit can be viewed during Agincourt branch open hours. Please chec
 k the\nAgincourt\nbranch page before your visit.\nFree. All are welcome.\n
 Image credit: Natalia Ionescu\; watercolour and pencil\; "Canadian Spring
 "
LOCATION:Agincourt\, First floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a35437ff56bd86e00b7bfdc
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Memory and Aging: What's Normal\, What's Not\, and What You Can Do
  About It
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260819T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260819T140000
UID:6a6a647e4523092f00337ded@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join us as psychologist Dr. Nicole Anderson discusses the kind
 s of cognitive function changes that are a part of healthy aging\, and pro
 vides examples of normal memory slips\, and not so normal memory slips. Th
 is presentation discusses factors affecting cognition\, including medical 
 factors\, and everyday lifestyle factors\, then provides a number of memor
 y strategies that people can use to make their memory function better.\nTh
 is is a drop in program\, no registration is required.
LOCATION:Albion\, Located in the Auditorium
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a6a647e4523092f00337ded
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
