BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Let's Talk Depression: What It Is\, What It Isn't\, and What Helps
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260921T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260921T200000
UID:6a6bcaece30fe4845965bdc1@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Depression is one of the most common mental health conditions 
 in the world\; yet it remains widely misunderstood.\nFar more than ordinar
 y sadness or "feeling down\," clinical depression (also called major depre
 ssion) is a complex mood disorder shaped by a combination of factors\, inc
 luding genetic predisposition\, changes in brain chemistry\, life stress\,
  and personal history. For many people\, it can be debilitating\, persiste
 nt\, and difficult to recognize — both in ourselves and in those we care
  about.\nThis talk will focus on demystifying depression and offer practic
 al\, evidence-informed insights into understanding and addressing it.\nWha
 t to Expect:\nUnderstanding Depression:\nThis talk will explore what depre
 ssion actually is (and what it is not)\, and how to recognize when low moo
 d may be something more serious.\nDispelling Misinformation:\nAddressing c
 ommon myths and misconceptions that can prevent or delay people from seeki
 ng help.\nGetting Help:\nA discussion of practical ways to support ourselv
 es and others\, what to do when someone close to us is struggling\, and wh
 en it may be time to seek professional care.\nAbout Dr. Gerasimos Konstant
 inou\nDr. Gerasimos Konstantinou is a psychiatrist and researcher based in
  Toronto\, where he works at the Centre for Addiction and Mental Health (C
 AMH) and Toronto Western Hospital. He specializes in mood disorders\, part
 icularly depression\, and in advanced treatment approaches for people who 
 have not found relief through standard medications or therapy.\nHe is curr
 ently an Assistant Professor in the Department of Psychiatry at the Univer
 sity of Toronto and leads the Mood Disorders Clinic at CAMH\, which suppor
 ts individuals experiencing acute episodes of depression or other mood dis
 orders.\nThis program is presented by Toronto Public Library in collaborat
 ion with\nThe Centre for Addiction and Mental Health (CAMH)\n.\nYour Healt
 h Matters\nThis program is part of Toronto Public Library’s\nYour Health
  Matters\ninitiative\, which offers trusted health programs and resources 
 to help people make informed decisions about their health. Learn more by e
 xploring the\nYour Health Matters web page\n.\nReserve Your Spot Today!\nT
 his virtual Zoom event is free to join but registration is required. Once 
 registered\, you’ll receive an email with the Zoom link.\nWe can’t wai
 t to see you there!
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a6bcaece30fe4845965bdc1
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:NatureTO: Trees Around the Branch Tour
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261005T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261005T193000
UID:6a495489541102b7fa8a674a@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:The Riverdale branch and its neighbourhood is home to a vari
 ety of trees. Join the City of Toronto Urban Forestry staff to explore the
  natural world on a local neighbourhood tour. Learn how to recognize diffe
 rent species and fun facts about their cultural and ecological importance.
 \nChildren under 12 must be accompanied by a parent/caregiver.\nThe tour w
 ill start and end at the Riverdale Branch and will be guided by the City 
 of Toronto Urban Forestry staff. There may be uneven\, unpaved surfaces al
 ong the route. Please dress and prepare accordingly for this outdoor progr
 am (e.g. gloves/mittens\, hats\, sunscreen\, water\, comfortable shoes). T
 his program takes place rain or shine. In the event of severe inclement we
 ather\, registrants will be notified of an alternative date.\nThis program
  is part of TPL's\nOur Fragile Planet\nprogram series\, supported by Frien
 ds of Toronto Public Library\, South Chapter.​\nInformation Collection N
 otice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto 
 Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, a
 nd 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program registra
 tion\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles yo
 ur information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\
 n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Riverdale\, Room 1
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a495489541102b7fa8a674a
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
